{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway (CNO&TP) -- Cincinnati to Chattanooga -- plus the Alabama Great Southern Railroad (Chattanooga to Birmingham), the two northern pieces of the 'Queen and Crescent Route' (Cincinnati the 'Queen City', New Orleans the 'Crescent City'), a Southern Railway system route completing the second, western Cincinnati-New Orleans corridor alongside the already-mapped New York-Atlanta-New Orleans Crescent (see southern_railway_crescent.geojson). This feature stops at Birmingham rather than continuing to New Orleans: south of Birmingham the Queen & Crescent used the same Alabama Great Southern/New Orleans & Northeastern trackage via Meridian that the Crescent file already traces, so continuing this feature to New Orleans would just duplicate that line rather than add anything. The CNO&TP's Cincinnati-Chattanooga segment is nicknamed the 'Rathole Division' for its steep grades and (originally) 27 tunnels through the Kentucky/Tennessee mountains. Geometry traced through real track using OpenStreetMap data (Overpass API) and shortest-path graph search; traced length (482.2 mi Cincinnati-Birmingham) is consistent with the route's real curvy mountain mileage.", "source": "Wikipedia (Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway; Alabama Great Southern Railroad; New Orleans and Northeastern Railroad; Queen and Crescent Route; Queen and Crescent Limited; Royal Palm (train); Ponce de Leon (train))", "pulled": "20260818", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway / Alabama Great Southern Railroad / Southern Railway", "line": "Alabama Great Southern: Queen & Crescent Route", "id": "queen-crescent-cincinnati-birmingham", "color": "#006747", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 55, "speed_source": "estimated: mountainous 'Rathole Division' terrain slows the Cincinnati-Chattanooga segment; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1880, "end": 1926, "headway_min": 480, "note": "The CNO&TP completed Cincinnati-Chattanooga Feb 21 1880 (the 'Rathole Division'), joining the already-built Alabama Great Southern (Chattanooga-Birmingham, completed 1871); the 'Queen & Crescent Route' marketing name was adopted Jan 31 1881; through service to New Orleans itself didn't begin until the New Orleans & Northeastern opened Meridian-New Orleans in Nov 1883; general/named service; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway; Alabama Great Southern Railroad; New Orleans and Northeast\u2026"]}, {"start": 1926, "end": 1949, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Queen and Crescent Limited, an all-Pullman flagship Cincinnati-New Orleans train, ran 1926-1949; per contemporary accounts it was 'never a financial success'", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway; Alabama Great Southern Railroad; New Orleans and Northeast\u2026"]}, {"start": 1949, "end": 1970, "headway_min": 720, "note": "After the Queen and Crescent Limited's 1949 retirement, this segment continued to carry Southern Railway service including the Cincinnati-Jacksonville Royal Palm (1913-1970) and Ponce de Leon (1924-1968), both of which used this same Cincinnati-Chattanooga trackage before diverging toward Atlanta and Florida; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Cincinnati, New Orleans and Texas Pacific Railway; Alabama Great Southern Railroad; New Orleans and Northeast\u2026"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.230615, 33.913826], "to": [-86.382521, 33.800047], "chord_mi": 11.53, "traced_mi": 11.74, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["NS AGS North District (11.74 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 12 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-84.5215971, 39.0962014], [-84.5292825, 39.0977941], [-84.5307905, 39.1002428], [-84.5329123, 39.1013111], [-84.5432716, 39.1025516], [-84.5443066, 39.1041867], [-84.5431634, 39.106629], [-84.5418635, 39.1070674], [-84.5405381, 39.106668], [-84.5396675, 39.1039346], [-84.5428286, 39.0955669], [-84.5462682, 39.089781], [-84.5479109, 39.0850536], [-84.5534921, 39.079997], [-84.5548422, 39.0741848], [-84.5592248, 39.0686741], [-84.557746, 39.058634], [-84.558093, 39.057344], [-84.5606092, 39.0559378], [-84.565794, 39.056324], [-84.56724, 39.055437], [-84.569486, 39.052015], [-84.571203, 39.050708], [-84.573908, 39.050102], [-84.5784565, 39.0504688], [-84.5808738, 39.0489286], [-84.581166, 39.044228], [-84.584563, 39.035344], [-84.603955, 39.010279], [-84.607104, 38.9